===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|trebuchet}}, {{m|fro|trebuket}} et al. (modern {{m|fr|trébuchet}}), from {{m|fro|trebuchier||to overthrow, topple}}, from {{m|fro|tre-}} + {{m|fro|*buchier}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|buc||trunk of the body}}, from Old {{etyl|frk|en}} {{m|frk|*buk|*būk|belly, trunk, torso}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*būkaz||belly, abdomen, trunk}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰōw-||to blow, swell}}. Cognate with {{cog|goh|būh||belly}}, {{cog|ang|būc||belly, trunk}}. More at {{l|en|bouk}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈtɹɛbəʃɛt/|/ˈtɹɛb.jə.ʃeɪ/|/ˈtɹɛb.ju.ʃeɪ/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|trěb’yo͞o-shet}}, {{IPA|/ˈtɹɛb.juˌʃɛt/|/ˈtɹɛb.jəˌʃeɪ/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A medieval [[siege engine]] consisting of a large pivoting arm heavily weighted on one end. Considered to be the technological successor to the [[catapult]].
#: {{usex|en|Medieval '''trebuchets''' were capable of launching 90kg projectiles over distances of more than 300 meters, making them more powerful than most pre-modern types of catapult.}}
# A [[torture]] device for [[dunk]]ing suspected [[witch]]es by means of a [[chair]] attached to the end of a long pole.

